chronicling the white-knuckle thrills of  crab fishing and the harsh speeds of the  Bering Strait Deadliest Catch has been  one of Discovery Channel's hottest shows  for more than a decade that's time  enough to bury plenty of skeletons at  sea so here's a look at some salty  behind-the-scenes tales even diehard  fans may not know about Deadliest Catch  almost deadliest with all of the carnage  left in the wake of the deadliest catch  crab boats it may be surprising to find  out that the crab seasons followed by  the show the red king crab and the  Opilio crab don't traditionally have the  highest body counts for fishermen  according to The Oregonian that to be  his honor goes to the Dungeness crab  industry with a death rate twice that of  the Bering Sea  this is apparently due to two factors  one Dungeness boats don't need a Coast  Guard safety inspection to get permitted  and two the Dungeness industry still  operates on the Derby system which  allows for inexperienced captains and  small boats to incur high risk trying to  catch as much crab as they possibly can  the Discovery Channel apparently  realized the superlative flaw in the  show's title which they've been  corrected by launching their spinoff  series Deadliest Catch dungeon Cove  which documents you guessed it that  Dungeness crab industry arrest warrant  Jake Harris son of the late captain Phil  Harris has had several reported run-ins  with the law for drug abuse and driving  while intoxicated while it seemed like  Harris was getting a handle on his  personal issues things started going  back downhill fast  first Harris was attacked in an apparent  robbery while sharing a ride home from a  casino  according to Seattle's channel 7 news  Harris was badly beaten suffering a  cracked skull and brain trauma a few  months later TMZ reported that Harris  allegedly stole a woman's car in Phoenix  and was subsequently busted by police  for possession of crystal meth and xanax  Harris then failed to show up in court  to face the charges stemming from the  arrest and as of May 12 2017 as a bench  warrant out for his arrest  according to Fox News double jeopardy in  2016 sig Hansen's estranged daughter  from his first marriage  Melissa Eckstrom opened a civil suit  claiming that her father sexually abused  her  when she was 2 years old the case is  complicated  since Hansen was exonerated at the same  charges in 1990 during a contentious  divorce and custody case in which the  judge ruled that the abuse did not occur  they´ll Hansen relinquished parental  rights to Melissa  he claims her mother has continually  attempted to extort him Hansen told The  Seattle Times this is nothing more than  an old-fashioned shakedown it's a  completely frivolous lawsuit full of  lies that my ex-wife made up to take  away my daughter and still uses to try  to extort money from me it's blackmail  pain and the uber around the same time  the abuse allegations resurfaced sig  Hansen made news again for allegedly  flipping out on an uber driver according  to the Seattle Times Hansen and his  son-in-law spat on the drivers head and  the back of the driver's seat Hansen  also allegedly kicked the outside of the  rear of the passenger side of the  vehicle causing a dent Hansen was  arrested at his home after a drunken  confrontation with Seattle PD but later  apologized in multiple tweets for his  actions I am terribly sorry for my  behavior and I'm very embarrassed by it  I owe a bunch of people apologies first  and foremost to our uber driver who was  just trying to get his home safely I  hope I can make that apology in person I  have no excuse and accept responsibility  for my actions I'm Jefferson fig and I  approve this message  no get slamming murder Deadliest Catch  has tragically covered the deaths of  multiple crew members who died working  in violent storm conditions but tragedy  has also touched the show far from the  docks of Dutch Harbor and a seemingly  random incident associate producer  Joseph McMahon was gunned down outside  his California home after he went  outside to investigate a noise according  to CBS LA and if that wasn't bizarre  enough the suspect in the shooting was  found dead in his car that same day from  what appears to be a self-inflicted  gunshot wound according to the rap  dining woes with the irregular hours and  back-breaking shifts the crews have to  deal with meal times on a crabbing  vessel are few and far between in an  interview with the Miami New Times sig  Hansen explained on the boat you don't  know when you're going to eat again they  are no specified mealtimes fishing is  what dictates your meals and what you  can eat so your diet is horrifying  you're not eating at regular times it's  hell and the one thing they're  definitely not eating crap despite being  surrounded by the eight-legged  delicacies you will never see The  Deadliest Catch fisherman sitting around  a table sucking down crab meat dubbed  and melted butter crabs are the reason  they're out there risking their lives so  it would be pretty counterproductive to  continually put a dent in their profits  by eating the catch  cameraman peril a lot of reality shows  that feature tough characters and  grueling environments generally provide  an opportunity for the production team  to leave those elements behind at the  end of their shifts on Deadliest Catch  this is not the case according to  associate producer Ryan McNeil the  two-man video crews aboard the crab  boats don't have it easy not only do  they have to endure the same weather  conditions and rough seas as the  fishermen they have to do it all without  ever leaving the boat which means  they're stuck on deck with the crew for  the entire crab run which generally  lasts three to five weeks this leads to  an inevitable camaraderie between the  fishing and film crews according to  captain Wild Bill wichrowski who told  the a/v Club  I'd say 85% of the camera guys I had  ended up being buddies we actually spend  more time with the cameramen on the boat  than we do with our own families if you  think about it you're with them 24/7 and  you don't get to see your own family  that much climate chaos environmental  factors obviously play a huge part on  Deadliest Catch raging storms colossal  waves and frigid water are all hazards  the crab boat crews face on a daily  basis but on season 13  the crews plan to change tactics due to  climate change speaking with a Daily  Mail executive producer art Dekker  Watson jr. said that the rise in water  temperatures has forced some of the  boats to strike out for new territory  that is more dangerous because of fierce  storms and is further from rescue  workers if something goes wrong though  Watson omits the show is careful to  avoid the political controversy  surrounding climate change he does admit  that both the production staff and the  crabbing crews have a personal stake and  how the changing environment affects the  fishing industry as a whole  when something like this comes up it's  felt by all of them I love making this  show and so does the rest of my team  thanks for watching click the looper  icon to subscribe to our YouTube channel  let's check out all this cool stuff we  know you love to
 

